+# st feature additions
+
+This patch set adds the following features on top of the existing st in this
+repository. All behavior is configurable from `config.h` (and most options via
+Xresources too).
+
+## New key/mouse bindings (config.h)
+
+| Binding                          | Action                                   |
+|----------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
+| `Ctrl` (held) + pointer over URL | URL is underlined (hover highlight)      |
+| `Ctrl+Shift+Click` on a URL      | copy that URL to CLIPBOARD               |
+| `Ctrl+Shift+O`                   | copy the last URL on screen to CLIPBOARD |
+| `Ctrl+Shift+S`                   | select the URL under the pointer         |
+| `Ctrl+Shift+U`                   | open the last URL (existing)             |
+| `Ctrl+Click` on a URL            | open that URL (existing)                 |
+| `Shift+PageUp` / `Shift+PageDown`| scroll one page in scrollback            |
+| `Ctrl+Shift+Up` / `Ctrl+Shift+Down`| scroll one line in scrollback          |
+| `Alt+PageUp` / `Alt+PageDown`    | jump to previous/next shell prompt       |
+
+The old `Ctrl+Shift+Up`/`Ctrl+Shift+Down` key-table entries (`\033[1;6A` /
+`\033[1;6B`) were correct but are now shadowed by the scrollback shortcuts;
+applications that need the sequences can bind them without the `Ctrl` or use
+the terminal's application cursor-key mode.
+
+## Selection improvements
+
+* When a selection is finished with the mouse (or with `Ctrl+Shift+S`), it is
+  copied to the CLIPBOARD as well as PRIMARY (`autocopy`, default on).
+* `Ctrl+Shift+S` selects the URL under the pointer as a normal selection, so
+  it can be pasted with the usual shortcuts.
+
+## Visual bell
+
+* `visualbell` (default on): when `bellvolume` is 0 the bell flashes the
+  whole window instead of doing nothing.
+
+## Window opacity
+
+* `opacity` (default 0.95) sets `_NET_WM_WINDOW_OPACITY` on startup. Requires
+  a running compositor; set via `st.opacity` in Xresources (e.g. `0.8d`).
+
+## Prompt-to-prompt scrolling (OSC 133)
+
+`Alt+PageUp` / `Alt+PageDown` scroll to the previous/next shell prompt.
+Prompts are marked by the shell itself using OSC 133, so the shell must emit
+the markers. Two ready-made snippets are included:
+
+* `st-osc133.bash`: source it from `~/.bashrc`
+* `st-osc133.zsh`: source it from `~/.zshrc`
+
+Example (bash):
+
+    [ -f /path/to/st-osc133.bash ] && . /path/to/st-osc133.bash
+
+The snippets emit `OSC 133;A` before each prompt; the terminal records the
+absolute line and `promptup`/`promptdown` scroll to those marks.
+
+## Inline sixel images
+
+Full DCS sixel support is included, so image protocols such as
+`chafa --format symbols` with a sixel backend, `w3m -img`, `img2txt`, or
+`catimg` display inline images. Features:
+
+* sixel raster attributes (`"`), color palette definitions (`#`), HLS and RGB
+  color modes, repeat introducer (`!`), newline/`-`/`$` positioning, and
+  6-band pixel rendering;
+* images are anchored to absolute scrollback lines, so scrolling keeps them
+  in place relative to the text they belong to;
+* drawing is cached (color `<->` pixel mapping) and repainted on demand;
+* the image list is bounded (`SIXEL_MAX_IMAGES`, oldest images are dropped);
+* images are discarded on reset, resize and alt-screen switch.
+
+## Implementation notes
+
+* `Term.histabs` tracks how many lines were pushed into the scrollback ring;
+  `tabsline()` returns the absolute line of the top row. Prompt marks, sixel
+  anchoring and scrollback positioning all use absolute lines.
+* The hover highlight works by redrawing just the affected rows
+  (`tsetdirt`), so there is no full-screen repaint on every mouse move.
+* Pointer motion is always selected so the hover state can be tracked; mouse
+  reporting to applications is unaffected.
